1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,803
A paper fill is not a win.

2
00:00:02,803 --> 00:00:08,008
It is the moment a process becomes responsible for managing an open position.

3
00:00:08,008 --> 00:00:15,615
Today's case study uses KO, the NYSE ticker for The Coca-Cola Company, only because a broker-confirmed PAPER fill exists.

4
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:21,620
This is not a recommendation, a live-money result, or a claim about the company's profitability.

5
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:23,222
The position remains open.

6
00:00:23,222 --> 00:00:28,427
There is no exit fill, so there is no closed-trade outcome to celebrate.

7
00:00:28,427 --> 00:00:35,233
The real story is what had to be checked before entry and what protection had to follow.

8
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:42,536
The daily research showed an aligned trend structure and positive relative strength and momentum versus a broad-market reference.

9
00:00:42,536 --> 00:00:49,839
Liquidity and quoted spread were acceptable in the connected feed, and measured volatility stayed within the process limits.

10
00:00:49,839 --> 00:00:55,113
One important caveat: volume confirmation was weaker than required for full scoring credit.

11
00:00:55,113 --> 00:00:57,953
That did not disappear from the review.

12
00:00:57,953 --> 00:01:02,416
A setup score is a ranking of evidence, not a forecast.

13
00:01:02,416 --> 00:01:10,936
It does not prove that a company is profitable, that a stock will rise, or that the next trade will work.

14
00:01:10,936 --> 00:01:17,885
Before the entry, the first time check exposed a mismatch between the market clock and quote timestamps.

15
00:01:17,885 --> 00:01:19,111
The process paused.

16
00:01:19,111 --> 00:01:22,381
A fresh clock-and-quote comparison later aligned the references.

17
00:01:22,381 --> 00:01:26,877
Only then did the system recheck the latest completed intraday bars.

18
00:01:26,877 --> 00:01:36,278
The quote was above session VWAP, the latest completed short-interval closes were rising, and price had not moved too far from its reference.

19
00:01:36,278 --> 00:01:37,913
Incomplete candles were excluded.

20
00:01:37,913 --> 00:01:41,592
The lesson is not to trust a single screen.

21
00:01:41,592 --> 00:01:46,906
It is to make independent data sources agree before an order is considered.

22
00:01:46,906 --> 00:01:49,506
Position sizing was calculated before execution.

23
00:01:49,506 --> 00:02:02,506
The process compared three constraints: the amount that could be risked if the protective trigger were reached, the maximum portfolio exposure allowed for one position, and the cash actually available.

24
00:02:02,506 --> 00:02:04,673
The smallest permitted size won.

25
00:02:04,673 --> 00:02:09,006
This keeps a strong-looking setup from consuming too much capital.

26
00:02:09,006 --> 00:02:13,773
The exact account size, share count and order details stay private.

27
00:02:13,773 --> 00:02:23,739
A paper simulation can test whether the sizing rules are applied consistently, but it cannot guarantee a particular fill or a maximum loss.

28
00:02:23,739 --> 00:02:29,119
The entry was submitted as a paper limit order during the regular US session.

29
00:02:29,119 --> 00:02:32,961
The system did not count the submission as a trade.

30
00:02:32,961 --> 00:02:38,725
It waited for the broker to confirm the actual filled quantity and average fill price.

31
00:02:38,725 --> 00:02:47,178
After that confirmation, it submitted a separate native trailing stop for exactly the protected quantity and verified that the order was open.

32
00:02:47,178 --> 00:02:53,326
This separation matters because protection cannot be assumed to exist just because an entry was accepted.

33
00:02:53,326 --> 00:02:58,705
If the stop had failed, the unprotected quantity would have needed to be closed.

34
00:02:58,705 --> 00:03:04,729
A trailing stop follows the position's high-water mark and can move upward as the market advances.

35
00:03:04,729 --> 00:03:10,754
It is not a fixed profit target, and this process did not add a take-profit order.

36
00:03:10,754 --> 00:03:13,766
It is also not a guaranteed maximum loss.

37
00:03:13,766 --> 00:03:20,166
A fast move, a price gap or poor execution can produce a fill below the displayed stop.

38
00:03:20,166 --> 00:03:26,567
Trailing stops trigger during regular market hours, so their behavior outside the session must not be assumed.

39
00:03:26,567 --> 00:03:29,203
Protection is a control, not a promise.

40
00:03:29,203 --> 00:03:31,242
What would invalidate the setup?

41
00:03:31,242 --> 00:03:39,806
A loss of the trend structure, deterioration in relative strength, a failed intraday reference, unreliable data, or a triggered protective stop.

42
00:03:39,806 --> 00:03:45,923
The process does not average down or add simply because the position moves against it.

43
00:03:45,923 --> 00:03:52,041
At this review, the paper position remains open and the trailing stop is still present.

44
00:03:52,041 --> 00:03:54,080
There is no exit fill.

45
00:03:54,080 --> 00:04:02,644
That means no realized result can be reported, and an open mark-to-market change must never be presented as a closed-trade win.

46
00:04:02,644 --> 00:04:10,555
The practical lesson is that a confirmed fill starts the management phase; it does not finish the story.

47
00:04:10,555 --> 00:04:21,103
Record the evidence that existed at entry, verify protection, monitor the invalidation conditions, and report the actual exit only when the broker confirms it.

48
00:04:21,103 --> 00:04:25,937
Keep the symbol and case-study context separate from private account information.

49
00:04:25,937 --> 00:04:34,727
What matters most in an automated system: finding a candidate, or proving that every step after the fill is controlled?

50
00:04:34,727 --> 00:04:35,166
AMORDER.

51
00:04:35,166 --> 00:04:36,045
Evidence first.

52
00:04:36,045 --> 00:04:36,924
Risk always.

53
00:04:36,924 --> 00:04:37,803
No promises.
